// =============================================================================
// bdp.lumen — the Beads Protocol spec, authored as Lumen
// =============================================================================
//
// This single Lumen source is THE spec for BDP. It supersedes the prose/profile
// split (core.md / types / corelib): the toolchain projects it to the JSON Schema
// bundle, the OpenAPI catalog, Go interfaces, the reference, and the canonical CLI.
//
// Source of record: gastownhall/bdp @ main. Owner: the spec owner (Lumen + BDP).
//
// Status: FIRST consolidation of the base-mechanism design ruled 2026-11-12 (project
// lead). Landed to main TEMPORARILY, so the work is not lost, with a full review
// cycle (review council + the BDP reviewers) immediately after. core.md and the old
// profiles remain only for a few hours as reference. Markdown is GENERATED from this
// file to specs/generated/, not hand-written (see AGENTS.md).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §0. Rulings recorded 2026-11-12 (project lead) — departures from v0.2 core.
//     Recorded as dated rulings so they do not read as drift; the newer ruling wins.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. NOMINAL refinement types, on the LUMEN surface only. v0.2 core was "schema
//     only, no nominal types / raw data access." This design uses nominal types with
//     refinement — `Bead is resource`, `Channel is Bead`, `Operation is Bead`,
//     `AsyncResult is Bead` — so every resource kind shares one versioning/provenance/
//     graph model. ON THE WIRE a Resource still carries only `type` (a `ref<BeadType>`);
//     `schema` stays value-only (types have identity, schemas do not).
//  2. Identity is `ResourceId { scope?, id, rev? }` + phantom `ref<T>` (the ONE reference
//     form): `id` always present, `scope`/`rev` optional (the operating scope / current
//     revision). The wire URL is the projection (scope URL + id). The old URI / pinned-URI
//     `Reference` is GONE (ruling 13); ref variants are refinements (pinned/unpinned/
//     inscope/crossscope/webscope).
//  3. New `createdAt` on Bead — resource birth time, denormalized onto every
//     revision for temporal analysis. NOT provenance (that is per-revision).
//  4. Reshaped Request/Reply/Outcome — flat (no `context` wrapper), arguments as a
//     name/value list mirroring `parameters`, `cause`+`frame` correlation, and a
//     one-and-done source `Channel` for async. `Outcome` gains `canceled` (kill -9,
//     core); `timed_out` and graceful-cancel ride with the scheduler.
//  5. Result arms are `success`/`failure` (ruled 2026-11-13; the earlier `ok`/`err` reshape
//     is reverted). The Outcome DU remains the BDP core designer's to own.
//  6. Dispatch is IMPLICIT — the request routes to the target resource (bead for
//     CRUD, Operation for `call`, Channel for `pull`). No dispatch table.
//  7. `schema` structures (`declarationsSchema`, `operationSchema`) are explicit;
//     `schema` itself is a JSON-Schema-valued value, lowered specially on export.
//  8. The primitives are a SUBSET a service may offer; discovery advertises which,
//     and how each behaves. `patch` is not required of every service (one
//     without it offers update callables). Built-in data primitives are NOT
//     callables/Operations (ruled 2026-11-08); Operation is for DECLARED callables;
//     Channel is the former EventSource.
//  9. committedAt and inception are SERVER-supplied on write, never request input; a
//     Request's `provenance` is the caller's part only (principals/message/metadata).
// 10. createdAt (ruling 3) is OPEN with storage: server-supplied? = the first
//     revision's committedAt? its value after delete / bring-back? its value for a
//     replicated or imported row (storage may not know)? To settle with storage.
// 11. NOT folded here, carried in core.md as the review agenda: §13.2 HTTP binding
//     (~250 lines, mostly proposed) stays there for now, NOT dropped; §§10/12/14/15
//     (the 14 storage promises, the Problem-code list, the v0.1→v0.2 ownership
//     tables, the 42 open items cited by number) remain the record of who owns what.
// 12. LINKS REMOVED (project lead, 2026-11-13; branch spec/nolinks). A relationship is an
//     owned `ref<T>` property on the SOURCE bead — part of its revision state — not a `Link`
//     resource. The `link` kind and `LinkType` are gone; relationship typing is the
//     property's declared type (Types' job). The edges projection, families, traversal and
//     referential-integrity constraints are the GRAPH corelib capability (advertised), not
//     base. Supersedes the `Link` type, the `link` kind, and ruling 3's "Bead/Link".
// 13. ONE REFERENCE WORLD (project lead, 2026-11-13; branch spec/nolinks). `ref<T>` (the
//     3-part ResourceId, typed) is the only way one resource names another. The URI /
//     pinned-URI `Reference` is removed; `AbsoluteUri` survives only for non-ref URIs
//     (Problem type, Principal id). Also: `id<T>`→`ref<T>`; `ResourceId.scope`/`rev` optional;
//     ref refinements pinned/unpinned/inscope/crossscope/webscope; `ResourceIdentity` dropped
//     (ops return a `ref`); `batch`→`apply` (+ `eval` added, batch = sugar); `Web`→`Generic`;
//     `resource` is a type; Result arms `success`/`failure` (ruling 5); `update`→`patch`;
//     write primitives (create/put/patch/delete/cancel) return a pinned `ref`, not a body.
// 14. PRUNED TO PLAIN SCHEMAS (project lead, 2026-11-16; closing out v0.2). No surface
//     generics and no new `resource` construct — the Bead/representation split is expressed
//     with plain schemas. `Bead` IS the representation (the record `get` returns); `ref<Bead>`
//     is its identity, so `rep<T>` and `ResourceRep` are retired and `representation` =
//     `Bead | WebResource`. `kind` is ONE slash-path (head = category), retiring `resourceKind`
//     and `beadKind`; `beadType`→`type`. The `kind` write param is gone (the type fixes it).
//
// OPEN / parked (not in this file): RunEvent & FormulaRun (Lumen Formula pack —
// the model's test, not the spec); the scheduler (deadline, timeout, graceful
// cancel + compensation); the `/sys/` well-known-id scheme beyond `/sys/changes`;
// capability discovery; full filter / query / search / count / graph (corelib
// capabilities, advertised, not base); aliases & the Transfer profile;
// `canceled` HTTP status (unruled); the cause-naming reconcile; cancel cascade (left
// to the owner); delete-and-bring-back; the discovery schema + how to fetch it.
// =============================================================================



// =============================================================================
// §1–§6. The resource & bead model  (closed 2026-11-16)
// =============================================================================
// Every declaration carries a `##` anchor (addressable from bdp.lumen) and a
// `/// description:`. Normative design rules are `/// normative:` metadata on the
// shape they govern; the §3/§4 rule blocks are prose (they span the model and the
// Lumen Types layer rather than one wire shape).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §0. Charter — what this spec carries (and what it does not)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   #1  The format & semantics of bead schemas and operations.  ← THE protocol.
//   #2  A general resource model — ONLY so the export format and packages can
//       carry NON-bead resources (files / assets). Minimal footprint.
//   #3  How types & scopes are modeled in Lumen (the Types layer). NOT here.
//
// Rulings baked in (2026-11-16):
//  - A reference to ANY resource is a ResourceRef; the pin lives in `rev`
//    (one reference world — including the authority that names a type).
//  - `kind` is NOT stored — it is `type.id`, the single-`is` refinement path.
//  - `Bead` is a marker: refining it puts the subtype's fields into `body`
//    (the firewall); refining `Resource` directly adds a new resource CATEGORY.
//  - `union` is for CLOSED value-level sum types only; kind hierarchies are
//    OPEN single-inheritance refinement.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1. Identity
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Rev
/// description: A resource-version token — opaque by definition; compared for exact equality only, never inspected.
type Rev = string

## Digest
/// description: A content-addressed rev (sha256 / commit) — a Rev that is a digest.
/// normative: `Digest is Rev`, so a digest satisfies any `rev: Rev`; there is no Rev-vs-Digest split on the wire.
type Digest is Rev

## BdpScope
/// description: The URL of a BDP scope (a store).
type BdpScope = AbsoluteUri

## Scope
/// description: The namespace an `id` resolves in. Absent ⇒ the current (local) BDP scope.
/// normative: A Scope is exactly one of — absent (the local BDP scope); a BdpScope URL (another BDP store, `id` relative to it); or the literal `"uri"` (NOT a BDP scope — `id` is an absolute URI whose scheme resolves it).
type Scope =
    BdpScope
  | "uri"

## ResourceId
/// description: The identity of a resource — a scope, an id within it, and an optional revision.
type ResourceId {
  /// description: The namespace the `id` resolves in; absent ⇒ local.
  scope?: Scope
  /// description: The id, relative to `scope` (or the absolute URI when `scope` is `"uri"`).
  id:     string
  /// description: The specific revision; absent ⇒ the current one.
  rev?:   Rev
}

## ResourceRef
/// description: A reference to a resource is just its id.
type ResourceRef = ResourceId

## ref
/// description: A typed (phantom) reference — T is checker-only and erases on the wire.
type ref<T>      = ResourceRef

## pinned
/// description: A reference pinned to one exact revision (`rev` present).
/// normative: A pin is a local Rev or an external Digest; since `Digest is Rev`, both satisfy `rev: Rev`.
type pinned<T>   is ref<T> { rev: Rev }

## unpinned
/// description: An unpinned reference (`rev` absent) — floats to the current revision.
type unpinned<T> is ref<T> { rev: null }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2. Resource & Bead
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## PathString
/// description: A slash-delimited path — `"issue/task"` (bead) or `"text/plain"` (content).
type PathString = string

## Authority
/// description: The resource that NAMES a `type` — a package, or a registry like IANA. A ResourceRef, so the pin lives in its `rev`.
type Authority = ResourceRef

## PackageRef
/// description: A pinned reference to a Package resource — an Authority that is a package.
type PackageRef = ref<PackageBead>

## Resource
/// description: The universal shape of a versioned resource — bead or not. Only `id`, `type` and `body` are always present; the rest is optional.
/// normative: Refining `Resource` directly adds top-level envelope fields — a new resource CATEGORY (contrast `Bead<T>`, whose refinements go into `body`).
type Resource {
  /// description: The resource's identity.
  id:            ResourceId
  /// description: Who names `type` — a package (bead) or a registry like IANA (content). Absent ⇒ ad-hoc / duck-typed.
  authority?:    Authority
  /// description: The type PATH, resolved within `authority`'s namespace.
  type:          PathString
  /// description: The content — raw for a bare resource; narrowed to a schema by `Bead<T>`.
  body:          any
  /// description: Opaque per-resource sidecar the protocol does not interpret.
  metadata?:     { ...: any }
  /// description: Who caused this revision, how its period began, and when.
  provenance?:   Provenance
  /// description: The resource's birth time, denormalized onto every revision.
  createdAt?:    Timestamp
  /// description: How the resource is kept — durable, ephemeral, or intrinsic.
  storageClass?: StorageClass
}

## Bead
/// description: A bead is a Resource whose body is typed, and whose authority (when present) is a package.
/// normative: `Bead` is the MARKER. Refining it (directly or transitively) narrows `body` to the subtype's schema — the FIREWALL: a bead's domain fields live under `body`, never alongside the envelope. `Bead<T>` narrows exactly two fields: `authority` to a package ref, `body` to `T`.
/// normative: The authority DISCRIMINATES bead vs content — a package ref (versioned, pinned) ⇒ bead; a registry ref (IANA, stable) ⇒ content. `authority` is still optional, so a bead MAY be duck-typed (authority absent).
type Bead<T> is Resource {
  /// description: Narrowed to a package ref; still optional (a duck-typed bead omits it).
  authority?: PackageRef
  /// description: The typed body — the firewall. Narrows `Resource.body` (`any` → `T`).
  body:       T
}

// Content is a BARE Resource — no narrowing: `type` is a media type named by a
// registry, `body` is raw bytes/text. (Retires the old "WebResource"; see §6.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3. Authority, type, and resolution  (normative prose)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A resource's TYPE is fully qualified by the pair (authority, type):
//   bead:    authority = a package ref { scope:"uri", id:<package url>, rev:<pin> };
//            type      = a package-relative path, e.g. "issue/task".
//   content: authority = a registry ref { scope:"uri", id:<IANA url> };
//            type      = a media type, e.g. "text/plain".
// The authority is PINNED (its `rev` = the resolved digest/semver), so the type a
// resource was born against is frozen at inception and replays identically.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §4. The type system (Lumen Types layer — normative prose)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PATH = CHAIN. Bead subtypes use SINGLE inheritance; the `is` chain builds the
// element path, which IS `type.id`, which IS `kind`:
//   type Issue is Bead  { … }   → "issue"
//   type Task  is Issue { … }   → "issue/task"
// Only ONE `is` parent is allowed — that is what makes the path (a tree) unique.
//
// INHERITANCE (how a chain flattens to one effective schema):
//   - additive: a child has all parent fields plus its own
//   - narrowing-only override: narrow a type, or optional→required; never widen or
//     drop — preserves substitutability (ref<Issue> may hold a Task)
//   - a same-named child field must be a narrowing, else a diagnostic
//   - `abstract?` on a TypeDef ⇒ not directly instantiable (a bead must use a leaf)
//
// RESOLVER (Types-layer capability, NOT core): walk the pinned path base→leaf and
// merge per the rules — the path IS the plan. A pinned authority ⇒ the effective
// schema is immutable and cacheable. A cross-package parent is a `refines` ref the
// resolver follows across authorities.
//
// CROSS-MODULE: a foreign name never enters the path. A cross-module `is` makes the
// child a LOCAL root whose `refines` holds a resolved ref (identity carries a ref,
// not a name). Within a module kind == schema-ancestry; across modules kind stays
// local and "is-a" is a `refines`-graph query (a capability), not a kind-prefix scan.
//
// REFINEMENT vs UNION: refinement (`is`) = OPEN kind hierarchy, path-addressed;
// `union` = CLOSED value-level sum type (Result, Outcome).

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §5. The bead bodies — each XBead = Bead<its body type>
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## TypeBead
/// description: A bead whose body is a type definition.
type TypeBead      = Bead<TypeDef>
## OperationBead
/// description: A bead whose body is an operation descriptor.
type OperationBead = Bead<Operation>
## ChannelBead
/// description: A bead whose body is a channel descriptor.
type ChannelBead   = Bead<Channel>
## PackageBead
/// description: A bead whose body is a package.
type PackageBead   = Bead<PackageDef>

## ElementRef
/// description: A bundled reference to a package element — a package authority plus the element's relative path.
/// normative: `authority` is OPTIONAL — a same-package (relative) reference omits it, as `refines` does within a package. A resource's OWN type is this pair unbundled into its `authority` + `type` envelope fields; ElementRef is the standalone / cross-package form.
type ElementRef {
  /// description: The package that names the element; absent ⇒ relative to the current package.
  authority?: PackageRef
  /// description: The element's package-relative path.
  path:       ElementPath
}
## TypeRef
/// description: A reference to a type element.
type TypeRef      = ElementRef
## OperationRef
/// description: A reference to an operation element.
type OperationRef = ElementRef
## ChannelRef
/// description: A reference to a channel element.
type ChannelRef   = ElementRef
## ElementPath
/// description: A package-relative element path (the single-`is` chain).
type ElementPath  = PathString

## TypeDef
/// description: The body of a TypeBead — one node in the single-`is` refinement tree.
/// normative: A type's element path is parent-path ⊕ its own name. A same-package parent is the path prefix (implicit); `refines` is set only for a cross-package parent. The Types resolver flattens the chain base→leaf per the §4 inheritance rules.
type TypeDef {
  /// description: Cross-package parent only; a same-package parent is the path prefix. Null + no prefix ⇒ root.
  refines?:   TypeRef
  /// description: True ⇒ not directly instantiable; a bead must use a leaf.
  abstract?:  bool
  /// description: The body's declared properties; inherited ones merge per the rules.
  properties: namedDeclarations
}

## Operation
/// description: The body of an OperationBead — the call contract, the implementation, and the behavioural guarantees.
/// normative: The effect markers (`pure`/`safe`/`idempotent`/`atomic`) are three-valued — present = a KNOWN guarantee, absent = UNKNOWN. `pure` ⟹ `safe`; `pure:true` + `safe:false` is a rejectable contradiction. The Lumen surface callable keywords lower to these fields (`fn` ⇒ pure).
type Operation {
  /// description: The call contract.
  signature:       Signature
  /// description: The mounted AST. Present ⇒ STORED (ships in the pack) · absent ⇒ INTRINSIC (native).
  implementation?: any
  /// description: Known to neither read nor write external state.
  pure?:           bool
  /// description: Known not to write (reads allowed); pure ⟹ safe.
  safe?:           bool
  /// description: Known to be idempotent.
  idempotent?:     bool
  /// description: Known to commit as a single transaction. DEFERRED to the scheduler.
  atomic?:         bool
}

## Signature
/// description: An operation's call contract — its interface, kept separate from the implementation and the effect markers.
type Signature {
  /// description: The input record — named, typed, required-flagged parameter declarations.
  parameters?: namedDeclarations
  /// description: The success type (T).
  success?:    BeadValueSchema
  /// description: The declared failure type (E).
  failure?:    BeadValueSchema
  /// description: Async disposition — sync-only · caller-picks · async-only.
  async?:      "no" | "optional" | "required"
  /// description: Whether a successful call mints a resource → 201 + Location.
  creates?:    bool
}

## Channel
/// description: The body of a ChannelBead — its two event schemas, direction, and delivery characteristics.
/// normative: `direction` and the two schemas are mutually constraining — `source` ⟺ pullSchema set & pushSchema null; `sink` ⟺ pushSchema set & pullSchema null; `duplex` ⟺ both set.
type Channel {
  /// description: What `push` accepts; null on a source (pull-only).
  pushSchema?: BeadValueSchema
  /// description: What `pull` delivers; null on a sink (push-only).
  pullSchema?: BeadValueSchema
  /// description: The advertised facet — source · sink · duplex.
  direction:   "source" | "sink" | "duplex"
  /// description: How far back a cursor can resume.
  retention:   Retention
  /// description: Delivery cardinality — single or broadcast.
  cardinality: "single" | "broadcast"
}

## PackageDef
/// description: The body of a PackageBead — its declared version and its elements.
/// normative: A package IS a resource; its canonical identity is its own `id` ({ scope:"uri", id:<url>, rev:<pin> }) — exactly what beads stamp as their `authority`. It is NOT restated in the body.
type PackageDef {
  /// description: The version this package declares; refs pin to a resolved one via their `rev`.
  semVer:   SemVer
  /// description: Elements keyed by package-relative path; value = ANY resource — bead defs AND file assets.
  elements: { ...: Resource }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §6. Worked examples (wire representations)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// --- authoring (Lumen) ---
//   type Issue is Bead  { title: string, status: "open" | "closed" }   // → "issue"
//   type Task  is Issue { estimate?: number }                          // → "issue/task"
//   type Bug   is Issue { severity: "low" | "high" }                   // → "issue/bug"
//   type Memory     { statement: string }
//   type Spec       is Memory     { status: "draft" | "accepted" }     // → "memory/spec"
//   type Constraint is Memory     { applies: "always" | "conditional" }// → "memory/constraint"
//   type Policy     is Constraint { enforcement: "advisory" | "block" }// → "memory/constraint/policy"
//
// --- a bead on the wire (a task) ---
//   { id:        { id: "4821", rev: "r7" },                              // scope absent ⇒ local
//     authority: { scope: "uri", id: "https://pkg.gastownhall.dev/beads", rev: "sha256:9f…" },
//     type:      "issue/task",
//     body:      { title: "Wire the changefeed", status: "open", estimate: 3 },   // firewalled
//     metadata: {}, provenance: { … }, createdAt: "2026-11-16T18:04:12Z",
//     storageClass: Durable { versioned: true } }
//   // kind (derived) = type.id = "issue/task"
//
// --- a content resource on the wire (text/plain) — a bare Resource ---
//   { id:        { id: "assets/readme.txt", rev: "r1" },                 // scope absent ⇒ local
//     authority: { scope: "uri", id: "https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types" },
//     type:      "text/plain",
//     body:      "Hello, world.\n",                                      // raw, flat — no firewall
//     metadata: {}, provenance: { … }, createdAt: "2026-11-02T09:00:00Z",
//     storageClass: Durable { versioned: true } }
// =============================================================================



// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §5b. Carried-forward schema (supporting + op-support)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Types the model and the ops still reference. Most are unchanged from v0.2; the
// TYPE-VALUE schema (`BeadValueSchema`, `Kind`) is NOT yet reconciled to the new
// `ElementRef`/`PathString` world — that is the deferred "types" pass. The two
// aliases below keep the ops resolving until then.

// temporary alias — the TYPE-VALUE schema's ref arm still says `to: Kind`; reconciled in the types pass.
## Kind
type Kind = string
// what `get` returns / what an export line carries — now just a Resource.
## representation
type representation = Resource
// per-call opaque bag used by Request/Reply.
## Metadata
type Metadata = { ...: any }

## StorageClass
/// description: How a Resource is kept. Durable is replicated and exported; ephemeral is local and never exported; intrinsic is computed by the service, not stored (capability beads are intrinsic).
schema StorageClass = Durable { versioned: bool } | Ephemeral | Intrinsic

## Provenance
/// description: Who took part in the change that made a revision, how the Resource's present period began, and when. `previousRevision` is the before-image pointer — the basis for compensation — and rides in the post-image, so a ChangeEvent carries it for free.
schema Provenance = {
  /// description: ```
  The principals who took part in the change. May be empty for observed or baseline revisions.
  ```
  principals: [Principal],
  /// description: ```
  How the Resource's present period began — freshly created, imported, revived after a delete, or unknown.
  ```
  inception: "create" | "import" | "revive" | "unknown",
  /// description: ```
  When this revision committed; server-supplied, never request input.
  ```
  committedAt: Timestamp,
  /// description: ```
  The before-image pointer — the basis for compensation. Null on create; structural lineage, often known even when the principals are not.
  ```
  previousRevision?: Rev,
  /// description: ```
  An optional free-text note describing the change.
  ```
  message?: string,
  /// description: ```
  Per-revision opaque data (DATA, open).
  ```
  metadata?: { ...: any }
}

## Principal
/// description: An identity that took part in a change — a person, agent or service account. A role grants no permission; authorization is a separate decision.
schema Principal = {
  /// description: ```
  The principal's identity URI. Need not be resolvable.
  ```
  id: AbsoluteUri,
  /// description: ```
  A saved human label — never identity, never a matching key.
  ```
  displayName?: string,
  /// description: ```
  Open set of roles this principal played in the change (initiator, contributor, submitter, …). A role grants no permission.
  ```
  roles?: [string],
  /// description: ```
  How the service established this identity.
  ```
  basis: IdentityBasis,
  /// description: ```
  The service that channel-authenticated this identity.
  ```
  authority?: AbsoluteUri
}

## IdentityBasis
/// description: How a service established a principal's identity.
schema IdentityBasis = union {
  /// description: ```
  The writer asserted the identity; the service did not verify it.
  ```
  "writer-supplied"
  /// description: ```
  The channel authenticated the identity when the change arrived.
  ```
  "channel-authenticated"
  /// description: ```
  The basis is not recorded.
  ```
  "unknown"
}

## AbsoluteUri
/// description: Any absolute URI — a Problem type, a Principal identity; need not resolve. NOT a resource reference (those are `ref<T>`).
/// jsonschema = { format = "uri" }
schema AbsoluteUri = string

## Timestamp
/// description: RFC 3339 date-time; uppercase T and Z.
/// jsonschema = { format = "date-time" }
schema Timestamp = string

## SemVer
type SemVer = string

## BeadValueSchema
/// description: A type written down as a value. A `schema` has NO identity, so it is a value. A `schema` IS a
/// normative: BeadValueSchema — the ref-preserving type representation. On EXPORT it lowers to a JSON Schema document
/// normative: (and an OpenAPI contract) for external consumers; the exported/wire form is byte-identical to the
/// normative: pre-BeadValueSchema JSON Schema, so the v0.2.0 freeze is NOT reopened. Only genuinely open DATA uses
/// normative: `{ ...: any }`; a written-down TYPE uses `schema`.
/// review-todo: the `TypeRef { to: Kind }` arm must be reconciled to the new `ElementRef` (authority? + path) in the deferred types pass.
schema BeadValueSchema =
    Scalar  { of: "string" | "number" | "bool" | "bytes" }
  | Literal { value: any }
  | Nominal { name: string, base: BeadValueSchema, refinement?: { ...: any } }
  | Record  { fields: [ { name: string, type: BeadValueSchema, required: bool } ], open: bool }
  | DU      { arms: [ { tag: string, payload?: BeadValueSchema } ] }
  | Union   { options: [ BeadValueSchema ] }
  | Array   { element: BeadValueSchema }
  | Set     { element: BeadValueSchema, on?: [ LocalName ] }
  | TypeRef { to: Kind }
  | Opaque  { jsonSchema: JsonSchema }

## LocalName
/// description: A short identifier token for a view or a well-known name; leading letter.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$" }
schema LocalName = string

## JsonSchema
/// description: A JSON Schema document — the lowered/exported form of a `schema` value for external (non-BDP) consumers.
schema JsonSchema { text: string }

## namedDeclarations
/// description: A list of named, typed declarations with required-ness — the shape of a record (a parameter list, a Type's property definitions).
schema namedDeclarations = [ { name: string, schema: BeadValueSchema, required: bool } ]

## Retention
/// description: How far back a cursor can resume; below the floor a cursor faults `cursor-expired` and the reader resyncs (list-bootstrap + follow from head).
schema Retention =
    forever
  | window    { by: "time" | "count" | "size", amount: number }
  | compacted { window?: Retention }

// --- op-support: correlation ids, request/reply, result/outcome/problem ---------
// (op-side; reworked with the ops.)

## IdempotencyKey
/// description: Caller-chosen key that makes a retried write safe to collapse.
/// jsonschema = { pattern = "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,256}$" }
schema IdempotencyKey = string

## Cause
type Cause       is string
## Frame
type Frame       is string
## Transaction
type Transaction is string

## Request
/// description: What a caller sends. `arguments` is a name/value list whose shape the Operation's `parameters` dictate.
schema Request = {
  arguments:      [ { name: string, value: any } ],
  async:          bool = false,
  causedBy?:      Cause,
  calledBy?:      Frame,
  provenance?:    Provenance,
  idempotencyKey: IdempotencyKey,
  metadata?:      Metadata
}

## Reply
/// description: What a `call` returns — flat, no context wrapper. `outcome` keeps `Result` nested in `Outcome`.
schema Reply = {
  outcome:      Outcome<any, any>,
  cause:        Cause,
  frame:        Frame,
  transaction?: Transaction,
  metadata?:    Metadata
}

## Result
/// description: A callable's own verdict — succeeded with a value, or failed with its DECLARED error.
type Result<T, E> = union {
  // Succeeded, carrying the value.
  success { value: T }
  // Failed with the DECLARED error (E).
  failure { error: E }
}

## Outcome
/// description: What happened to an invocation — the domain Result, or a non-domain fate. OPEN: a reader MUST tolerate an unknown arm.
type Outcome<T, E> = union {
  // The domain Result — success | failure.
  Result<T, E>
  // An undeclared failure (NOT an E) — carries a Problem.
  fault { problem: Problem }
  // kill -9 — runs no compensation. `timed_out` is a cancel reason, with the scheduler.
  canceled { reason?: string }
}

## Problem
/// description: An RFC 9457 problem document — what a `fault` carries, and the body of a failing response from the data primitives.
schema Problem = {
  type:      AbsoluteUri,
  title:     string,
  status:    number,
  code:      string,
  retry:     "never" | "after-state-change" | "after-delay",
  detail?:   string,
  instance?: AbsoluteUri
}

## AsyncResult
/// description: The async result of one `call` — a handle you show for the record and `cancel` to kill. References a one-and-done source Channel that fires the Reply on settlement.
schema AsyncResult = {
  operation:    ref<OperationBead>,
  cause:        Cause,
  frame:        Frame,
  request:      Request,
  reply?:       Reply,
  replyChannel: ref<ChannelBead>
}
## AsyncResultBead
type AsyncResultBead = Bead<AsyncResult>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §7. The operations — the mandatory set (canonical CLI + HTTP)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Dispatch is implicit: the request routes to the target resource. Content kinds
// (bead) takes client CRUD; the Operation takes `call`; the Channel takes `pull`.
// Conformance tiers: minimal/read-only = get + list + changefeed pull + discovery;
// full = + create/put/patch/delete + apply + call/cancel + export/import.
// Advertised in discovery (per-scope, not the floor): eval + its expression language
// (query / traversal / predicates), search, count, graph.
// Precision principle: spec the MOST precise form, relax by capability. Write primitives
// return `pinned<resource>`; a scope without revisions (StorageClass not versioned) relaxes
// all its refs to `unpinned`, advertised in discovery.

// --- Content resource: the Bead takes this set ---

## get
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "get" }
/// description: Safe point read — resolve a `ref` to the current representation, or a pinned revision when the ref pins `rev` (or `?revision=`). An optional `selector` projects the result (its own cacheable identity). Faults 404/410 when absent or erased.
/// openapi = { operationId = "get", method = "GET", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { revision = { in = "query", schema = "Rev", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,404,406,410] } }
fn get(
    resource: ref<Resource>,
    selector?: selector)
    -> representation

## IdRoot
schema IdRoot { prefix: string}
## Id
schema Id { id: string}


## create
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "create" }
/// description: Admit a new Bead. Caller-proposed id optional; fail-if-exists. Returns the created resource's pinned `ref` (rev = the minted revision); its URL in `Location`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "create", method = "POST", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 201 }, responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } }, problem = { status = [400,409,422] } }
command create(
    scope: Scope,
    requestedId?: IdRoot | Id ,                // caller-proposed identity (no rev yet)
    type?:        any,                              // the bead's type; its variant fixes beadKind
    properties?: { ...: any },                      // relationships are owned ref<T> props here
    metadata?:   { ...: any }
  ) ->
  Result<pinned<Resource>,  Problem>

## put
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "put" }
/// description: Upsert / remember — idempotent full-state write. Create-or-replace; the lifecycle effect (create vs update) is what the changefeed reports. Returns the written pinned `ref` (rev = the new revision).
/// openapi = { operationId = "put", method = "PUT", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifMatch = { in = "header", schema = "Rev", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,409,412,422] } }
idempotent command put(
    resource: unpinned<Resource>,
    id?:          unpinned<Resoure>,                // caller-proposed identity (no rev yet)
    type?:        any,                              // the bead's type; its variant fixes beadKind
    properties?: { ...: any },                      // relationships are owned ref<T> props here
    metadata?:   { ...: any }
    ifRevision?: Rev
  ) -> 
    Result<pinned<Resource>, Problem> 

## patch
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "patch" }
/// description: JSON Patch limited to /properties and /metadata. Optional `--if-revision` (If-Match) gives optimistic concurrency; a stale revision faults `revision-mismatch`. Returns the patched resource's pinned `ref` (rev = the new revision). (JSON Patch is the base format; a richer patch format is an advertised capability.)
/// openapi = { operationId = "patch", method = "PATCH", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifMatch = { in = "header", schema = "Rev", required = false } }, request = { contentType = "application/json-patch+json" }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,404,409,412,422] } }
command patch(
      resource: unpinned<Resorce>, 
      patch: JsonPatch, 
      ifRevision?: Rev) -> 
      Result<pinned<resource>, Problem>


## delete
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "delete" }
/// description: Retire a Resource, minting a final revision. Idempotent. Inbound references are owned by other beads' revisions, so they may dangle; a service that enforces referential integrity faults when inbound references pin it (advertised, not base). Returns the final pinned `ref` (rev = the revision it minted), not a representation.
/// openapi = { operationId = "delete", method = "DELETE", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], params = { ifMatch = { in = "header", schema = "Rev", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,404,409,412] } }
idempotent command delete(
          resource: unpinned<Resource>, 
          ifRevision?: Rev) 
          -> Result<pinned<resource>, Problem> 

## list
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "list" }
/// description: A collection of CURRENT resources in a scope, as a source<representation>. `where` is a ListFilter (the flat low bar every service supports). Baseline read; the full `filter`/`query` is a corelib capability. Snapshot — `--format json|jsonl`, no `sse`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "list", method = "GET", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,406] } }
fn list(scope: Scope, 
          where?: ListFilter, 
          select?: selector, 
          page?: Page) -> 
          source<representation> //// no result<T, E>

## revisions
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "revisions" }
/// description: The history of ONE resource — id-targeted like `show`, list-shaped like `list`. `select` controls payload (ids-only vs full representations). Snapshot.
/// openapi = { operationId = "revisions", method = "GET", pathItem = "resource", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,404] } }
fn revisions(
          resource: unpinned<Resource>, 
          select?: selector, 
          page?: Page) -> 
          source<representation> /// no Result<T>

// --- Invoke: call an Operation, cancel its run ---

## call
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "call" }
/// description: Invoke an Operation. Sync (default) returns the Outcome directly (200 / 201+Location when `creates` / 409 on `err` / Problem status on `fault`). `--async` returns 202 + the AsyncResult at Location; settle later. The ONLY primitive with the rich Outcome.
/// openapi = { operationId = "call", method = "POST", pathItem = "operation", tags = ["Core"], params = { async = { in = "query", schema = "bool", required = false } }, responses = [ { status = 200, schema = "Reply", description = "sync ok" }, { status = 201, schema = "Reply", description = "sync ok, mints a resource" }, { status = 409, schema = "Reply", description = "declared err — branch on the arm" }, { status = 202, schema = "AsyncResult", description = "async — result at Location" } ], responseHeaders = { Location = { schema = "AbsoluteHttpUrl" } } }
fn call(
        operation: ref<Resource>, 
        request: Request) -> 
        Reply //// no result<T,E>? 

## cancel
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "cancel" }
/// description: kill -9 on a running AsyncResult — writes reply.outcome = canceled. Runs NO compensation (that is the scheduler's graceful path); its effects stay findable via `causedBy`.
/// openapi = { operationId = "cancel", method = "POST", pathItem = "asyncResult", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [404,409] } }
command cancel(
    asyncResult: unpinned<resource>) -> 
    Result<pinned<AsyncResult>, Problem>


## Page
/// description: Windowed paging request — resume an opaque cursor xor start at a known ordinal, with a limit.
schema Page {
    after?: Cursor, 
    from?: Ordinal, 
    limit?: number 
}


## pull
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "pull" }
/// description: Read a Channel — the changefeed (well-known `/sys/changes`) or a declared stream — as a source of its `pullSchema` events. Valid on a `source`/`duplex` channel; a `sink`-only channel faults `channel-not-pullable`. `--format` picks the transport (json windowed / jsonl bulk / sse live); one Cursor spans all three. Faults `cursor-expired` below the retention floor.
/// openapi = { operationId = "pull", method = "GET", pathItem = "channel", tags = ["Core"], params = { after = { in = "query", schema = "Cursor", required = false }, from = { in = "query", schema = "Ordinal", required = false }, limit = { in = "query", schema = "number", required = false }, format = { in = "header", schema = "Format", required = false } }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,405,410] } }
fn pull(
    channel: unpinned<Channel>, 
    request: PullRequest) -> 
    PullPage //// no result

// --- Emit: push to a Channel ---

## push
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "push" }
/// description: Write a Channel — consume a source of events (the channel's `pushSchema` type) and append them in order. The dual of `pull`, and the channel analog of scope `import`: skip-and-report under a PushStrategy, idempotent re-push. `jsonl` body (bulk); `sse` is pull-only. Valid on a `sink`/`duplex` channel; a `source`-only channel faults `channel-not-pushable`. A terminal (closed/failed) channel faults; a `single` channel caps the batch at one.
/// openapi = { operationId = "push", method = "POST", pathItem = "channel", tags = ["Core"], params = { strategy = { in = "query", schema = "PushStrategy", required = false } }, request = { contentType = "application/jsonl" }, success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,405,409,422] } }
command push(
  channel: unpinned<Resource>, 
  source: source<any>, 
  strategy?: PushStrategy) -> 
  Result<PushReport, Problem>

## PushStrategy
/// description: How `push` handles a rejected event or a re-push — the event analog of ImportStrategy (distinct: events, not resources).
schema PushStrategy { 
    dedup?: bool, 
    dryRun?: bool 
}

## PushReport
/// description: What `push` reports — counts and any skipped/rejected events by ordinal. The event analog of ImportReport (distinct).
schema PushReport { 
    pushed: number, 
    skipped: number, 
    conflicts: [ { ordinal: Ordinal, reason: string } ] 
  }

// --- Scope-level: atomic apply (write-set), pure eval (query), bulk export/import ---
/// I think we agreed these get out of core.  Import/Export pack, apply goes with batch, eval goes wiht query


## export
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "export" }
/// description: Bulk read of a scope as JSONL — current state by default; `--revisions` emits every revision in order (a true backup: createdAt, every committedAt, the previousRevision chain survive). Selection ⊆ ListFilter. Reads from the authoritative revision store, so it is a backup even when the live changefeed is compacted. Consistent as of a cut Cursor.
/// openapi = { operationId = "export", method = "GET", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200, contentType = "application/jsonl" }, problem = { status = [400] } }
fn export(
    scope: Scope, 
    where?: ListFilter, 
    select?: selector, 
    revisions?: bool) -> 
    source<ExportRecord> //// no errir?

## import
/// bdp = { kind = "primitive", primitive = "import" }
/// description: The bulk WRITE path — put/create under If-Match with id remap; a history file is replayed in commit order to rebuild the chain (history-restore), else it sets current state. Not a filter op.
/// openapi = { operationId = "import", method = "POST", pathItem = "scope", tags = ["Core"], success = { status = 200 }, problem = { status = [400,409,422] } }
command import(
    scope: Scope, 
    source: source<ExportRecord>, 
    strategy?: ImportStrategy) -> 
    Result<ImportReport, Problem>

// --- Operation request/patch bodies & read-shaping ---

## JsonPatchOp
/// description: One RFC 6902 operation. In `patch`, paths are limited to /properties and /metadata.
schema JsonPatchOp = { op: "add"|"remove"|"replace"|"move"|"copy"|"test", path: string, value?: any, from?: string }

## JsonPatch
/// description: An ordered list of JSON Patch operations — the `patch` body, and a change-event patch payload.
schema JsonPatch = [ JsonPatchOp ]

## selector
/// description: A read projection over a representation — include/omit subtrees (JSON Pointer) and expand. Query-string, so a projected read has its own cacheable identity; `kind`/`id` are never dropped.
schema selector { 
    include?: [JsonPointer], 
    omit?: [JsonPointer], 
    expand?: [LocalName] 
}

## JsonPointer
/// description: A JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) into a representation.
type JsonPointer is string

## ListFilter
/// description: The low-bar list filter — a flat conjunction (AND) of simple field conditions, exactly the query-string-expressible set. The baseline every service supports. The full boolean/traversal `filter` is a corelib capability (query), not base.
schema ListFilter = [ listCondition ]

## listCondition
schema listCondition = union
    compare     { field: FieldPath, op: "eq"|"ne"|"lt"|"le"|"gt"|"ge"|"in", value: any }
    matches     { field: FieldPath, pattern: string, using: "substring"|"glob"|"regex" }
    has         { field: FieldPath }
    missing     { field: FieldPath }
    contains    { field: FieldPath, value: any }
    containsAny { field: FieldPath, values: [any] }
    excludes    { field: FieldPath, value: any }
}

>>> I think there's a great question about isType vs isKind.  If we still want isType, exact goes away. I think there is some value in donig a good treatment of kind - kind of a variation of exact - prefix match vs. complete match.
>L applied (your call) — isKind, `match: "exact" | "prefix"` (no "type" for kinds; minimal semantic weight). prefix = kind-path ⊑ (ltree `<@` / `LIKE 'bead/…%'`), exact = `=`. Default `prefix` (the common "is it an issue(-ish)" test). Broader "type→kind" rename (Bead.type / BeadType / subjectType) is the kinds thrust.
  | isKind      { kind: RelativeId, match: "exact" | "prefix" = "prefix" }
## FieldPath
/// description: A field path as a segment array (disambiguates dotted keys); selector/patch keep JSON Pointer.
schema FieldPath = [string]
## RelativeId
/// description: A kind-path as a local id segment, relative to the scope (the target for isKind's prefix/exact match).
schema RelativeId = string


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §8. Change events & the changefeed
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each Scope has one built-in changefeed Channel (source; pullSchema = ChangeEvent) at the well-known
// id `/sys/changes`. Its events are what create/put/patch/delete commit.

>>> uh, what about the revision and provenance of the delete?  Maybe the arm of this needs a little love
>L PROPOSAL (per "name the tombstone; put provenance in the DU; maybe a top-level DU") — ChangeEvent is now a TOP-LEVEL DU on lifecycle (Created | Updated | Deleted); `op` disappears (the arm IS the lifecycle). Common fields factor into ChangeEnvelope, spread into each arm. Provenance is placed per-arm, ELIDED where the post-image already carries it (Created / Updated-full → the representation's Bead.provenance) and EXPLICIT where there is none (Updated-patch / Deleted). `subject.rev` is always the new revision — the TOMBSTONE revision on Deleted. Review this shape.
## ChangeEvent
/// description: Core's report of one committed change to one Resource, as a top-level DU on lifecycle (a relationship change is an Updated to its source bead). `subject.rev` is the new (or tombstone) revision. Provenance rides in the post-image where there is one and is explicit where there is not.
schema ChangeEvent = Created | Updated | Deleted

## ChangeEnvelope
/// description: Fields common to every change arm; spread into each. `subject.rev` IS the new (or tombstone) revision — there is no separate revision field.
schema ChangeEnvelope = {
  ordinal:      Ordinal,                          // position in the changefeed, assigned on append
  at:           Timestamp,
  subject:      ResourceId,                        // { scope, id, rev } — rev IS the new (or tombstone) revision
  subjectKind?: Kind,
  subjectType?: ref<BeadType>,
  transaction:  Transaction,                        // shared across one commit
  cause?:       Cause,
  causedBy?:    Cause,                               // parent cause — the cause-tree back-pointer
  frame?:       Frame,                              // diagnostic-tier
  calledBy?:    Frame                               // diagnostic-tier — the call-tree back-pointer
}

## Created
/// description: A resource admitted. Full post-image; its Bead.provenance IS the provenance.
schema Created = { ...ChangeEnvelope, representation: representation }

## Updated
/// description: A resource changed. `content` is the orthogonal ENCODING axis — `full` carries a post-image (provenance rides in it), `patch` is a delta with explicit provenance.
schema Updated = { ...ChangeEnvelope, content: ChangeContent }

## Deleted
/// description: A resource retired — the TOMBSTONE. No post-image, so provenance is explicit; `subject.rev` is the tombstone revision.
schema Deleted = { ...ChangeEnvelope, provenance: Provenance }

## ChangeContent
/// description: The change payload for Updated — full-vs-patch, the ENCODING axis (orthogonal to lifecycle).
schema ChangeContent =
    full  { representation: representation }          // a post-image (put/replace or a full update); provenance rides in it
  | patch { patch: JsonPatch, provenance: Provenance } // a PATCH delta; no post-image → provenance explicit


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §9. Pull & the transport model
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One source<T>; `--format` picks the projection; one Cursor hands off between
// them. SSE is gated to LIVE sources (pull); a snapshot (list/export) has nothing
// to follow. A "live list" is list(snapshot) + changefeed-follow(sse) — a
// composition / advertised capability, not a mode of `list`.
//
//   | --format | Accept:                | valid on            |
//   | json     | application/json        | pull, list, export  |  windowed pages (default)
//   | jsonl    | application/jsonl       | pull, list, export  |  bulk
//   | sse      | text/event-stream       | pull only (live)    |  live

## PullRequest
/// description: The input to `pull` — which stream, where to resume, how much, and the transport. `after` (a cursor) xor `from` (a fresh ordinal) are mutually exclusive.
schema PullRequest = {
  >>> uh, isn't the source the subject and first parameter?  I *think* we elevate teh target resource to a distinct first parameter
  >L applied — correct, `source` was a duplicate: `pull(channel, request)` already takes the Channel as the distinct first parameter. Removed it; PullRequest now carries only resume/transport/filter/select, matching how every op takes its target as the first param and never re-states it in the body. (Changefeed-by-default is a property of the `channel` argument — the well-known /sys/changes.)
  after?:   Cursor,
  from?:    Ordinal,
  limit?:   number,
  format?:  Format,
  filter?:  ListFilter,                             // filter the stream
  select?:  selector                                // project each event
}

## Ordinal
/// description: An event's position in its stream; monotone within one Channel.
schema Ordinal = number

## Cursor
/// description: Opaque, transport-independent resume token, bound to one stream and the selection it came from; refused elsewhere. Also the point an export is cut at.
schema Cursor = string

## Format
/// description: Transport projection a caller selects (the `--format` arm): windowed JSON pages, bulk JSONL, or live SSE. `sse` is valid only on a live source (pull); a snapshot (list/export) refuses it.
schema Format = "json" | "jsonl" | "sse"



## PullPage
/// description: One finite page of a stream — the windowed floor every service offers: one request, one page, nothing held open.
schema PullPage = {
  /// since we use pullpages for things other than events, maybe call this field "items" or something like that?
  >L applied — renamed `events` → `items` (a Channel's E is often not an "event", e.g. a declared stream).
  items: [ any ],                                   // the Channel's E (ChangeEvent for the changefeed; items generally)
  cursor: Cursor,                                   // where to resume; opaque, bound to this stream + selection
  head?:  bool                                      // caught up to the live edge?
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §10. Export format
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JSON Lines: a begin record, one Resource record per line (literally the result
// of `get` — a full representation, or every revision under `--revisions`), then
// an end record. A reader REFUSES a file that ends without the end record.

## ExportRecord
/// description: One line of an export.
schema ExportRecord = ExportBegin | representation | ExportEnd

## ExportBegin
/// description: The opening line — the Scope and the cursor the export was cut at (where a consumer resumes the changefeed). Told apart from a Resource line by `kind`. (Spelling proposed.)
schema ExportBegin = { kind: "export-begin", scope: AbsoluteHttpUrl, cursor: Cursor }

## ExportEnd
/// description: The closing line — a count of the Resource records, so a truncated file (one ending without this line) is detectable and never taken for whole. (Spelling proposed.)
schema ExportEnd = { kind: "export-end", count: number }

/// description: How `import` merges — strategy and id remap (mirrors v0.1 bd import flags).

>DB what is sourcePrefix?
>L nuked (your call) — removed; it'll come back if we actually need it.
## ImportStrategy
schema ImportStrategy = { dedup?: bool, allowStale?: bool, dryRun?: bool, idRoot?: string }

## ImportReport
/// description: What `import` reports — counts and any skipped/conflicted records.
schema ImportReport = { imported: number, skipped: number, conflicts: [ { id: ref<resource>, reason: string } ] }


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §11. Well-known resources
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The request routes to the target resource (implicit dispatch); well-known ids
// let a client address system resources by convention.
//   /sys/changes   — the scope's changefeed Channel (source; pullSchema = ChangeEvent)
//
// Well-known Types a client can recognise from a Bead's `type` alone (fixed URLs
// OPEN): BeadType (the type of a type), Channel, Operation, AsyncResult.
// What each MEANS is Types' job; Core speaks only where a Type's content is one of
// its own envelopes (AsyncResult holds a Reply).


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Appendix — the `///` backend metadata vocabulary
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One namespaced record per backend; the single top-level key is the namespace —
// no dotted keys. `title:`/`description:` are plain strings consumed by every
// backend. Backends: schema bundle, OpenAPI, Go, reference, CLI.
//   bdp        — protocol markers: { kind = "primitive", primitive = "get", id = "<uri>" }
//   openapi    — projection control: { operationId, method, pathItem, tags, params,
//                paramGroups, request, success, problem, responses, responseHeaders }
//   jsonschema — base-type refinements: { format }, { pattern }, { minLength }, { closed }
//   doc        — reference-rendering mode: { style = "http" | "rpc" }
//
// requestContext → BDP-Cause / BDP-Provenance / BDP-Metadata request headers;
// replyContext   → BDP-Cause / BDP-Transaction / BDP-Metadata reply headers.
// =============================================================================
